OPTIMIZED INSTRUCTIONS ARE A DIFFERENT NAMESPACE.

The standard 256-row inventory describes portable DEX input. A reserved byte
is not an instruction which the reader may execute as a harmless placeholder.
An ART cache, VDEX file, Dalvik odex image or historical runtime revision
may use a different instruction namespace. Its runtime version and container
must be established before interpreting its bytes. The byte alone is not
enough, even if an independent decoder has a familiar-looking name for it.

The independently maintained dexlib2 Opcode.java snapshot under
_/fixtures/dex/sources is the explicit historical evidence used here.
Its ODEX_ONLY marker and API/ART-version maps must not be discarded.
The portable catalogue does not grant these forms as standard instructions.

HISTORICAL DALVIK OPTIMIZATIONS.

The snapshot records volatile instance/static field accessor forms in
0xe3 through 0xeb and later object volatile forms at 0xfc through 0xfe,
with API bounds. These are runtime-specific accesses to fields with the
applicable volatile ordering. Portable DEX instead uses the ordinary field
instruction and the field's declared volatile property.

It records throw-verification-error at 0xed, execute-inline at 0xee,
execute-inline/range at 0xef, and version-dependent direct-empty or
object-initializer-range invocation at 0xf0. These are linked or specialized
runtime actions, not new portable ordinary calls. In particular the operands
of inline formats select a runtime inline table, not a method_ids entry.

The historical quick accessors at 0xf2 through 0xf7 use resolved field offsets.
Virtual quick calls at 0xf8/0xf9 and historical super quick calls at 0xfa/0xfb
use runtime table positions. Their embedded numbers are not portable symbolic
field or method indices. Runtime/layout-specific linkage metadata is needed
to recover the original symbolic references safely.

HISTORICAL ART QUICKENING.

The same decoder maps return-void-barrier and return-void-no-barrier to
0x73 under different ART-version bounds. It maps quick field forms across
0xe3 through 0xe8, virtual quick calls to 0xe9/0xea and narrow quick field
forms across 0xeb through 0xf2. These differ from the Dalvik assignments.
Return barrier choices concern implementation synchronization work and do
not allocate a portable return opcode at 0x73.

NUMERICAL COLLISIONS.

In portable DEX 038, 0xfa and 0xfb are polymorphic calls and 0xfc/0xfd
are custom calls. From 039, 0xfe and 0xff load method handles/types.
Those meanings do not replace optimized meanings in an older odex namespace.
Conversely an old decoder's optimized name must not override a modern
standard DEX interpretation after the file version and namespace are known.

The source checker compares the PORTABLE, non-ODEX entries with the main
inventory. It separately preserves the entire foreign enum, including
historical range maps. It does not pretend this note enumerates every private
optimized opcode ever used by every runtime vendor. Unknown optimized bytes
remain unsupported and require their own pinned implementation source.

The suggested optimized formats 20bc, 22cs, 35ms/3rms and 35mi/3rmi in
the AOSP formats document support interpretation of such evidence; they
do not require a portable runtime to accept optimized code as input.
